Some of them are probably true.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.800
And some of these are a terrible idea.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.120
I think AI generated tests

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.840
also a terrible idea.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.840
So I'd like to explore all of these things
in a bit more detail.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:09.800
And there's quite a lot of nuance in this,
because there are aspects of these

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.200
where they're all true.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.200
But let's talk about it.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.280
So first of all, I'd like to I'm a I'm

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.520
a old school card carrying, test driven
development developer.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:25.480
So let's start with tests because that's
where you're supposed to start.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.160
What's a test for?

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:30.480
Is it to prove success? Not really.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.640
Is it to find our mistakes?

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.200
Well, kind of, but not really.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:38.520
Is it to challenge
my genius as a programmer?

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.160
Certainly not.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:40.720
It's not.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.720
Those are not really what tests are for.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.720
They're not what? Make tests useful.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.520
I would argue that what tests are for us.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:52.920
They are a form of measurement.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.960
They are the equivalent of a carpenter
having a tape measure

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:59.080
in his pocket
that he can measure things with.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:04.960
We use tests to figure out
that we're doing the right things.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:09.320
We use tests to figure out that
when we've done them, they continue to be

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:14.040
the right things, their measurements,
to see if we've achieved our goals.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.880
But for that, we've got to define
what our goals are,

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.000
and we can't even further

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.280
the goals from the solution
because the solution can always be wrong.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.760
So anybody that's selling
you free AI testing, that will come in

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:33.600
and look at your existing system
and test it.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.520
That might have some value,
but it's not the same value

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:41.640
as proper testing that we need
as part of a development process.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:43.440
It's a different thing.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:47.760
And the reason for that is fairly
obviously, if I, if I write a function

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:50.960
something like this, calculate tax,

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:55.920
and I'm going to return 50 times
the amount that I put in.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:59.600
That's a very bad place to leave
if that's what your tax looks like.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:04.240
But if that's my starting point,
the only thing that an agent of AI

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.520
or any other kind of AI can do
is look at that and say, okay,

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:11.520
here's my test.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.480
It's just going to reinforce the wrongness
that I wrote into that function.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.480
All that a test can do at that point,
because the only input it's got

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.000
is the test is the is the working code
is verified that the working code does

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.360
what the working code does
that has its use uses.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:30.360
That's very good.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.640
If you want to refactor

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:35.520
the code, do behavior
preserving change and not break anything.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:36.880
But it's rubbish.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.880
If you want to develop a new system
and verify that this new system

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.960
continues to do or does the right things,
because now it's proving

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.080
that it does the wrong thing
because that's where we started from.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.640
So that's not giving us our ability
and ability

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.080
to define what we really wanted.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.680
AI generated tests.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:02.680
If the code is the only input, we can only
verify that the code remains the same.

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:07.800
We can't infer the goals from the solution
because the solution can always

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:08.680
be wrong.

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:16.120
So they're mostly a dumb idea.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.760
They have a
they have a place, but mostly a dumb idea.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.200
They tend to be a cop out

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.200
for people who don't can't be bothered
to state their goals.

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:25.680
And I think that's a problem

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.200
because specifying
the goals is kind of what our job is.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:34.160
It's kind of what we're here to do
as software developers.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:41.200
My next question what's the program for?

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:44.640
To define a sequence of instructions.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:47.800
Well not really. That's not the goal.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:53.040
It might be what it is, but to encode
algorithms, that's not the goal.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:55.000
Again of of a program.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:56.400
That's not what we do it for.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.400
That's not the value that it brings

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.840
to implement our brilliant design.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:03.560
Again,

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:08.440
that's some of the self-fulfillment
that I as a programmer might get from it.

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:11.400
But that's not the goal.
That's that's not the goal.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.440
That's not the reason
that people pay me to do it.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:16.800
So the

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.280
commercial
pressure is not none of those things.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:21.800
That's not really what it's for.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:25.560
As much as we might might like
to think of it in that way as programmers.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:30.200
Programing languages,
I would argue, have three goals as tools.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:33.760
So first, they are tools

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:38.840
that have been designed to help us
to organize our thinking about a problem.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.240
They allow us to explore the surface
area of a problem

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:45.840
and kind of understand it
in more detail, in more depth,

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:47.960
and they're designed to work that way.

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:51.760
Programing languages aren't difficult
because we want it

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:53.680
to be obscure in a truce.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:59.240
We wanted tools that would help us to
explore the problems in these kind of way.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:02.520
They're designed to help us to do that.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:04.880
They're also a means of

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:08.720
communicating our understanding
with other programmers or the humans.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:12.880
So they are communication tool between us,
so we can work as part of a team

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.680
and understand
what we are in some technical detail,

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:20.560
exploring the depth and the breadth
and the complexity of a of

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:24.000
of the problem that we're trying to solve
and the solution

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.680
that we've come up with encoded
as a program in a programming language.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:33.640
And ultimately they're there
to tell a computer what to do.

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:37.000
But that's kind of the last part,
because assembly language

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:40.280
instructions inform a computer
what to do perfectly well.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:43.840
And we not many of us, spend our time
writing programs

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:46.840
in assembly language.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:56.200
There are also three techniques
that are embodied in programing.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:09:00.400
Languages that are think are important
for us to think about and to remember

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.720
when we're talking
about programing as a discipline.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:08.520
The first is
we have a simple, consistent grammar.

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:12.560
It allows us to express our ideas

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:16.320
in a concrete, specific, precise way,

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:20.080
precisely enough
to be executable by a computer.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:23.920
They are an unambiguous expression

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:27.840
of our intent in that respect
and that this, this matters.

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:31.800
And they're repeatable and deterministic
in terms of execution.

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:33.600
And this matters a lot too.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.800
If we write something
in the programming language of our choice

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.880
and run it twice, given the same inputs,
we're going to get the same result.

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:47.880
But every time that's important,
it means that we can reason about it.

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.080
It means that we can test it.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:52.040
It means that
we can understand what it means,

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:56.080
and we can design systems that are more
or less repeatable in that sense.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:59.080
But the tools themselves are repeatable.

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:03.680
How does natural language
measure up to that?

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:08.960
Because natural language, somebody said
today, I think they quoted Andrei

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:13.360
Karpathy saying that the programing
language of the future is English.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.960
I don't like that idea very much
for the reasons that I'm talking about.

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:21.080
It's not precise enough, it's too vague.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:24.120
Natural language helps us.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:25.120
How does that measure up?

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:28.120
Helps us to organize our thinking
about a problem?

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:31.480
Well, it's too vague, really.

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:34.920
Natural language
is open to misinterpretation.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:36.280
If anybody's been around.

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:37.880
I can't really see you very well.

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:42.080
But if you've got a gray beard like mine
and you've been around enough,

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:48.400
I bet that you've been in a situation
where somebody coming down from on high

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:53.040
and given a very vague instruction
to a bunch of programmers and gone away

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:55.200
and then been really disappointed
in the results

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:58.400
because they didn't tell us in
enough detail, that's really common.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:02.880
It's really easy to misinterpret
what the goal really is.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:07.720
Natural

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:10.840
language is always open to interpretation.

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:12.720
Time flies like an arrow.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:13.920
These are some time flies.

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.920
Liking arrows.

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:23.040
Vibe coding alone is simply not
good enough.

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:27.960
If we're just chatting with the computer
to express our needs, that's not enough.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:29.200
We need tools

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:33.760
that allow us to be more precise
than that, to be more specific than that,

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:37.000
to be better prompts of what it is
that we want to achieve.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:41.800
That doesn't mean
that we need to look at all the code,

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:45.440
and are only allowed to do this
in a programming language.

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:50.160
I'm I'm in the category of its
been a long time now since I've.

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:55.920
So I've written any code by hand
because my AI agent writes all the code,

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:01.120
but I've been more precise than just
natural language in some respects.

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:05.720
In specifying what I want from it,
I'm able to get what I want from it

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:07.560
by using a precise,

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:11.440
prescriptive version of natural language
in order to be able to prompt it.

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:14.280
And that's
what I'm really talking about here.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:18.720
So does it communicate our understanding
with other humans?

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:21.320
It's a bit too

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:24.640
ambiguous to do that very well,
because as we

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:28.560
as we all know, does it tell a computer
what we want it to do?

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:30.400
It's too imprecise for that.

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:33.560
So it's very easy
unless what we're telling it

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:40.000
is something very obvious, like asking our
AI assistant to implement a buzz for us.

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:44.080
It might do that in a repeated way,
but otherwise it's probably not.

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:45.440
It's going to be a little bit different

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:48.440
and give us something
a little bit different every time.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:51.440
Certainly in the earlier versions

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:54.880
of AI coding assistance,
we've all seen that.

00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:58.320
Where we've asked system
to build something,

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:01.440
you ask it the same thing again
and you get something very different.

00:13:03.640 --> 00:13:05.920
That certainly happened to me a few times.

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:13.440
So we've got these.

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:16.000
What about these three techniques
that we have?

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:19.000
Simple, consistent formal grammar.

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:22.320
Does natural language give us that?

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:25.560
No. It's complex and inconsistent.

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:28.120
Unambiguous expression of intent.

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:30.160
No, it's ambiguous and vague.

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:33.160
Repeatable. Deterministic.

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:33.960
Not repeatable.

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:36.040
Not deterministic,
not version controllable.

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:41.440
As a result, we can if we version control
the language that we prompted real with.

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:45.200
That's not enough to get back
to the same result again

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:48.760
on its own.

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:50.640
So programing with a

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:54.520
AI presents three important problems

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:57.960
for us to replace traditional programing.

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:03.200
So how do we specify what we want
with precision.

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:06.240
That's problem one.

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:11.120
Problem two is how do we confirm that
we got what we wanted.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:14.160
This is the verification problem.

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:17.880
And problem three is that we don't work

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:21.200
in the same way as the machines.

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:26.320
They don't work in the same way
as us either way, which you look at that,

00:14:26.360 --> 00:14:30.200
the way that we do things
is that we tend to deal

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:34.040
with problems with,
with the limits of our own context window.

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:36.560
So what will fit inside
our heads at a time?

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:39.880
We might have a picture of what it is
that we want to build, but

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:43.200
then small change by small change.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:45.800
The way that we deal with it
is that we do incrementally.

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:49.400
We do a small piece of work,
we evaluate that piece of work,

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:53.040
and then we then we move on
to the next small piece of work.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:57.640
I would argue that high quality system

00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:01.960
development in software is always

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:05.360
an incremental process
of learning and discovery.

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:09.120
We are always exploring the problem space

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:13.080
and trying to understand it
over time, bit by bit by bit.

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:14.200
I'd go further than that.

00:15:14.200 --> 00:15:16.640
I would say that's all engineering.

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:20.640
All engineering is a is an act of

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:23.840
incrementally improving on

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:27.440
what we know about the problem
and dealing with the

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:31.400
with the new things that we learn
as we do that over time.

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:34.600
And that's a hard part of software

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:37.600
development,
a hard part of engineering in general.

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:42.320
And then we've got to verify
that we got what we wanted.

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:46.640
And it does all of the things that we need
in a repeatable, reliable way.

00:15:46.760 --> 00:15:50.080
And that's a hard
part of software development as well.

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:53.120
And what have we done?

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:54.560
We sped up the coding bit.

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:58.400
That was the easy part of software
development, which is good.

00:15:58.440 --> 00:15:59.240
It's great.

00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:04.720
It does accelerate us,
but it also moves the bottleneck.

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:08.680
If you've ever read the goal, the Theory
of Constraints, that's what we've done.

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:11.240
We've just moved the bottleneck
to somewhere else.

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:13.680
And actually
we probably haven't moved the bottleneck

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:17.800
because the bottleneck was nearly always
about verification and release, rather,

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:21.520
and figuring out what we really wanted
rather than the encoding of it

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:25.080
if we were any good as delivery agents.

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:31.000
So an AI works differently and AI works.

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:33.760
It kind of builds
this context, this picture,

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:37.880
and then it goes bang
and it makes a huge change.

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:40.320
Probably

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:43.200
until recently,
you kind of rewrite the whole system

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:46.240
every time, and there's
nothing really to stop it while it's

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:50.480
building your stuck control system
to write Space Invaders instead,

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:55.000
if we haven't told it not to,
if we haven't defined what that goals are

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:58.360
and are in a position
where we're able to evaluate it.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:01.400
Now, part of the problem here

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:06.200
is that a lot of people
that talk about using AI,

00:17:06.520 --> 00:17:10.120
AI's, and agents to help with coding

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:14.320
are often talking from a perspective
of building small, simple systems.

00:17:14.360 --> 00:17:18.280
I don't know about all of you,
but those aren't the sorts of systems

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:20.360
that I generally build professionally.

00:17:20.360 --> 00:17:23.080
They're certainly how I've played
with the A's.

00:17:23.080 --> 00:17:26.440
I've written Space Invaders
with an AI agent,

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:30.760
and I've done simple things like that,
all those nice, straightforward things.

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:33.760
But my professional building of software

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:37.480
has been for bigger,
more complicated systems than that.

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:39.040
And that's not good enough.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:42.920
Just just being able to check them
manually to see if they're okay.

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:48.280
He's not even close to being good enough
to verify that we get what we wanted.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:53.640
So if the AI is

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:57.480
making huge steps
and changing things in one big,

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:02.880
big, big change at a time,
and we're not able to verify

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:06.160
that we got what we wanted effectively,
how do we learn?

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:07.960
How do we find our understanding?

00:18:07.960 --> 00:18:11.160
How do we reliably update our solution
to improve it?

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:15.880
And how do we regenerate if it's
regenerating it from scratch every time?

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:17.360
We can't.

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:21.560
It's not really helping us to learn
and adapt and figure out where we are

00:18:21.560 --> 00:18:24.560
and figure out what we need to do next.

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:28.800
It's it's a problem.

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:33.120
The last two problems, problems two

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:37.440
and three mean that we losing our ability
to work incrementally.

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:42.320
And that is fundamental to building

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:47.000
complex systems of any kind,
but certainly complex software systems.

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:49.920
It's this process
of learning and discovery.

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:53.560
So how do we keep our ability
to learn, discover

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:57.000
and incrementally add to our systems
as we go?

00:18:57.120 --> 00:19:00.360
We have to have we have to have a means

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:04.480
that can keep up with the rate
at which we can produce software.

00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:10.600
I spoke to somebody not very long ago
who, who's who's engaged wholeheartedly

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:15.120
in the adoption of agents, programing,
using multiple agents to build systems.

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:16.440
He's writing a game.

00:19:16.440 --> 00:19:20.880
He reckons
he he writes 12,000 lines of code per day.

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:23.000
No human being

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:26.880
can review 12,000 lines of code per day.

00:19:26.920 --> 00:19:30.880
No human being can test manually test

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:33.960
the output of 12,000 lines a day
behaviorally,

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:36.040
to figure out
whether it's doing the right things.

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:37.800
We've got to find ways

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:41.760
of speeding up the rate of verification
and assurance to tell

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:46.960
whether we got what we wanted to keep up
with that speed of generation of code.

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:50.880
It's the only thing that makes sense,
it seems to me, if we're not to suffer

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:54.680
from the theory of constraints problem.

00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:00.880
How do we confirm

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:03.960
then that we always get what we want?

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:09.160
So first we've got to specify
what we want, clearly reproduce

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:13.200
in a reproducible manner,
and then we've got to be able to verify

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:17.480
repeatedly
that we still have it after every change.

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:20.880
If you're familiar with my work

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:24.480
with continuous delivery
and so on, that might sound familiar.

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:26.440
It's because it is.

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:30.360
It is the same that we've been doing
when we're talking about practicing

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:32.560
continuous delivery. It's table stakes.

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:36.520
We have to have this fast
cycle of verifying

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:39.520
that we got what we want.

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:43.240
So how should programing

00:20:43.240 --> 00:20:46.240
change to keep up with all of this?

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:51.680
So problem one specifying what we want
with precision.

00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:55.440
In the past,
the way that we did this was that a

00:20:55.440 --> 00:20:59.840
program was a precise solution
encoded as algorithms.

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:03.280
Weirdly,
we kind of lost what the problem was.

00:21:03.280 --> 00:21:07.520
It's kind of implicit in our solution,
but we'd keep that in our heads and we'd

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:13.920
build a solution, and that would be a
definition that we'd work to.

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:18.840
But the solution itself was implicit.

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:24.280
So in this in my example,
this is a routing algorithm.

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:27.280
So I'd like to find my way
home is the outcome

00:21:27.280 --> 00:21:30.280
that I'm trying to achieve here.

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:36.800
In the future

00:21:36.800 --> 00:21:41.880
a program will be a precise description
of what it is that we want,

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:47.560
I think encoded as specifications,
translated into executable instructions

00:21:47.560 --> 00:21:51.760
by the AI that will verify
that we got what we wanted

00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:57.440
and what we want now is explicitly part of
if you like the program.

00:21:57.440 --> 00:22:04.280
So the program moves away
from being solution focus to being

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:08.200
a more accurate description of the problem
that we're trying to solve.

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:12.160
I'm describing
a form of spectrum development,

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:17.320
but the specification takes the form
of executable specifications tests.

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:23.240
If you like that, both specify what
we want and also verify that we got it.

00:22:28.360 --> 00:22:30.960
What it takes to achieve that program.

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:35.200
I've just just repeating what I said
designing a domain specific language,

00:22:35.200 --> 00:22:36.840
if you like, for specifying

00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:40.600
what it is that we want from our software
with a deal of precision

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:45.960
and then specifying what we aim
to achieve in detail all of the time.

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:49.240
And those those are all of the behaviors
of the system,

00:22:50.080 --> 00:22:53.960
not just the conventional user behaviors
that we might think about.

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:54.920
We're not talking about

00:22:54.920 --> 00:22:58.520
sketchy testing of the system,
but all of the behaviors of the system.

00:22:58.520 --> 00:23:01.120
So how fast would we like
the results back?

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:04.120
If performance matters,
how secure do we want it to be?

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:06.560
If we want secure our system to be secure?

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:10.600
Because all of those are contextual,
you can't have the AI to infer that

00:23:10.600 --> 00:23:13.600
to make it up, because it depends

00:23:13.640 --> 00:23:16.640
the real how security
you want your system to be.

00:23:16.840 --> 00:23:19.800
You'd be stupid to apply the level

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:23.760
of security to a single user game.

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:24.360
Then you would.

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:27.360
If you were building a system for a bank,
because you'd be over

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:30.720
engineering the game
and probably under engineering the bank.

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:35.080
So you need to be specific
about what it is that you're

00:23:35.080 --> 00:23:38.760
trying to achieve in all of the dimensions
of architecture and design.

00:23:38.760 --> 00:23:42.160
So some of those are behaviors
of the system that we need to specify to

00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:44.440
the second problem. How does it.

00:23:44.440 --> 00:23:45.400
How about that.

00:23:45.400 --> 00:23:49.040
So how do we confirm that
we got what we wanted in the past.

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:52.080
We hoped that our solution solved
the problem.

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:56.680
Language verified some aspects of this
in terms of the syntax that we use,

00:23:56.680 --> 00:23:59.840
particularly if we use typed languages
and stuff like that.

00:24:01.240 --> 00:24:02.360
But they didn't.

00:24:02.360 --> 00:24:05.560
It didn't work to verify
functional correctness.

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:09.280
If you were a well-behaved, test
driven development developer,

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:12.800
then testing would would fill that gap

00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:16.840
and would verify that our code did
what we wanted it to do from that,

00:24:17.080 --> 00:24:20.160
from that, in that sense,
but otherwise, maybe not.

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:23.920
In the future.

00:24:23.920 --> 00:24:26.560
Executable specifications

00:24:26.560 --> 00:24:30.120
will work
as the verification of our system,

00:24:30.120 --> 00:24:34.960
as well as the specification of what it is
that we aim to achieve.

00:24:35.560 --> 00:24:39.160
I think this makes the AI

00:24:39.400 --> 00:24:42.200
we can do this in in human language.

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:45.480
This doesn't have to be difficult or hard.

00:24:45.520 --> 00:24:50.560
The way that I do it is that I teach my AI
how to do the kind of

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:57.080
BDD style tests that I like to use,
and I specify what I want,

00:24:57.120 --> 00:25:00.880
and my AI fills in the gaps
from relatively simple

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:04.800
English descriptions of what it is,
what the specification that I that

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:06.080
of what I want.

00:25:08.320 --> 00:25:09.040
AI generates

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:12.040
solutions that match the specifications,

00:25:13.120 --> 00:25:16.120
and now we can run those tests
against those solutions.

00:25:16.120 --> 00:25:19.920
We can verify
that the ALS doing the right thing

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:23.280
by giving test test values
that it hasn't seen before.

00:25:23.280 --> 00:25:26.280
So it can't cheat the tests

00:25:26.920 --> 00:25:28.960
and get feedback that we got.

00:25:28.960 --> 00:25:33.680
We actually did get what we wanted,
what it takes to achieve that.

00:25:33.920 --> 00:25:36.920
The programmer.

00:25:37.520 --> 00:25:40.520
Invents problem specific DSL,

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:42.800
defines
all of the desired behaviors of the system

00:25:42.800 --> 00:25:49.640
as prompts to the AI in the form of BDD
style specifications, and the AI takes

00:25:49.640 --> 00:25:53.400
the specifications, builds test
infrastructure to make them executable.

00:25:53.680 --> 00:25:57.880
Generates code to fulfill
all of the executable specifications.

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:01.520
And we've got what we wanted.

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:05.480
We've got the the a precise definition
of the behaviors that we want

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:09.080
and a verification
that we got those behaviors.

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:14.920
Regaining the last of the three
regaining incrementalism.

00:26:15.080 --> 00:26:18.960
So in the past
we would work iteratively in small steps

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:21.960
adding new tests and code.

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:25.800
We would gather feedback
validated by our deployment pipelines,

00:26:26.120 --> 00:26:29.800
build systems incrementally
treat each changes, and experiment

00:26:29.800 --> 00:26:33.560
and value empirical
learning in the future.

00:26:36.920 --> 00:26:37.680
We're going to work

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:40.800
iteratively in small steps,
adding new tests and code.

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:44.600
The AI will generate the code,
gather feedback to validate

00:26:44.600 --> 00:26:47.600
every change.

00:26:48.400 --> 00:26:49.800
Build systems incrementally.

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:51.600
Many small changes, treat each changes,

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:53.400
and experiment
and value empirical learning.

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:54.880
So it's exactly the same.

00:26:54.880 --> 00:26:58.200
So the stuff if you've been practicing
continuous delivery

00:26:58.960 --> 00:27:02.000
as two of the speakers that I heard

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:05.000
speaking today said,

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:09.000
at least implicitly,

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:14.080
then things become easier
because that's what we're doing.

00:27:14.080 --> 00:27:15.240
The same things.

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:19.720
We're evaluating stuff as we go, working
in small steps, and we're understanding

00:27:19.720 --> 00:27:23.680
where we are at all times,
and so are our AI agents.

00:27:24.360 --> 00:27:27.680
What does this take to regain
the incrementalism in this sense?

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:31.080
Well, we version control
the specifications because those are now

00:27:31.240 --> 00:27:34.240
in effect program.

00:27:34.560 --> 00:27:38.320
We run the test
cells in a deployment pipeline.

00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:40.560
Otherwise the AI is going to cheat.

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:42.880
And we're going

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:45.920
to stop the AI gaming the tests
by using the sorts of techniques

00:27:45.920 --> 00:27:49.760
that I mentioned about maybe keeping
some tests back and not showing the AI

00:27:50.520 --> 00:27:54.160
for what's going to what's,
so it can't cheat them.

00:27:55.480 --> 00:27:57.280
Most of this that I've described

00:27:57.280 --> 00:28:01.360
is how acceptance testing with behavior
driven development already works.

00:28:01.360 --> 00:28:04.000
So this isn't anything necessarily new.

00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:07.600
If you've been working this way before,
this is a really simple

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:10.600
step.

00:28:11.680 --> 00:28:14.920
Here's an example that I've used.

00:28:14.960 --> 00:28:20.760
I've done this now several times,
many times for building real systems.

00:28:20.760 --> 00:28:22.320
This is not a real system.

00:28:22.320 --> 00:28:22.760
This is

00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:27.000
this is an example from a training course
that I that I did an exercise with.

00:28:27.160 --> 00:28:31.400
So here are some the kinds
of specifications that I'm talking about.

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:34.600
If you think about
what I'm trying to describe,

00:28:34.600 --> 00:28:36.880
we start off with a vague
wish of what we want.

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:42.440
We get to a slightly more formal version
of describing that which is a user story,

00:28:42.440 --> 00:28:44.080
and then we come up with some examples

00:28:44.080 --> 00:28:48.160
that would demonstrate that the feature
that the story describes exists.

00:28:48.160 --> 00:28:49.440
Those are examples.

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:51.560
Those are our executable specifications.

00:28:51.560 --> 00:28:53.560
That's
what these things are on the screen.

00:28:53.560 --> 00:28:57.880
These are the ones that prompted
building a system that actually did this.

00:28:57.920 --> 00:29:00.720
It actually
did flight planning in this way.

00:29:05.040 --> 00:29:06.680
My conclusions are

00:29:06.680 --> 00:29:10.120
that natural language alone is not good
enough.

00:29:11.480 --> 00:29:13.640
BDD domain specific

00:29:13.640 --> 00:29:17.640
languages are the best alternative
for prompting effectively,

00:29:17.640 --> 00:29:22.000
and giving us the combination
of precise specification of what we want

00:29:22.040 --> 00:29:25.720
and built in verification that we got it.

00:29:26.840 --> 00:29:31.240
Automating testing from a solution
is a bit of a joke.

00:29:31.280 --> 00:29:33.320
It's it's a niche.

00:29:33.320 --> 00:29:36.800
It has some utility,
but it's a corner case.

00:29:36.800 --> 00:29:39.800
It doesn't solve the real problem.

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:42.160
We should treat skills to analyze

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:45.320
and decompose problems
as the real core of our discipline.

00:29:45.320 --> 00:29:50.880
That's the real discipline
that we as engineers technologists bring.

00:29:50.920 --> 00:29:56.560
It's that way of reasoning about a problem
of decomposing it and thinking

00:29:56.560 --> 00:30:01.600
about the corner cases, thinking
about the kind of the architecture level.

00:30:01.640 --> 00:30:06.160
One of the ways of thinking about all of
this is that effectively what we're doing

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:11.680
is we're involved in invoking
a fifth generation programming language,

00:30:11.720 --> 00:30:15.960
a fifth generation programming system,
and I think that's where we're at.

00:30:16.840 --> 00:30:19.840
So we need a different model
for the role of AI.

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:22.440
AI assistance is rather like the compiler.

00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:27.120
We're not going to care
for very much longer at all if we do. Now

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:30.880
about the code that it generates, because
we'll verify that we got the results.

00:30:30.880 --> 00:30:34.040
And as long as we get the results,
who cares about the implementation?

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:38.520
It's rather like the move
from assembly language programing

00:30:38.560 --> 00:30:40.280
to high level language programing.

00:30:40.280 --> 00:30:40.960
I don't know

00:30:40.960 --> 00:30:45.080
if anybody in the room is old enough
to remember that I, I don't quite,

00:30:45.080 --> 00:30:49.680
but I was an assembly language programmer
for a while, and for a while

00:30:50.280 --> 00:30:51.160
I used to

00:30:51.160 --> 00:30:55.120
look at what the assembly language was
that was generated by my compilers,

00:30:55.120 --> 00:30:59.520
but I haven't done that for years
and most people have never done that.

00:30:59.520 --> 00:31:02.960
So why should we care about what
the code that was generated was?

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:06.520
If we can verify that the behaviors
that we wanted are the ones that we got.

00:31:09.920 --> 00:31:12.200
If you'd like
to see a demonstration of this in use,

00:31:12.200 --> 00:31:15.520
there's an open source project called NYSE
that's worth a

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:20.240
look that imply
that applies this thinking

00:31:20.240 --> 00:31:23.240
and promotes this way of development.

00:31:37.920 --> 00:31:40.920
So that's.

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:45.120
I'm going to skip over here because I got

00:31:45.360 --> 00:31:46.000
where am I.

